//? Title: Plan interim analyses with group-sequential boundaries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Spend the type I error across looks

Testing accumulating data at several interim analyses with an unadjusted critical value inflates the type I error. Four looks at \(z\ge1.96\) give an overall one-sided error of 6.3% instead of 2.5%. A group-sequential design raises the boundary at each look so that the probability of ever crossing under the null equals \(\alpha\). In the Lan–DeMets approach, a spending function \(\alpha(t)\) fixes how much error is used by information fraction \(t\). Boundary \(b_k\) then solves
\[P_0(Z_1<b_1,\dots,Z_{k-1}<b_{k-1},Z_k\ge b_k)=\alpha(t_k)-\alpha(t_{k-1}).\]
Because only the spending function is fixed in advance, the number and timing of looks can change during the trial.

`group_sequential_boundaries` computes the boundaries for O'Brien–Fleming-type, Pocock-type, power and Hwang–Shih–DeCani spending. It evaluates the multivariate normal probabilities with the Armitage–McPherson–Rowe recursion and Simpson's rule. `boundary_crossing_probabilities` gives the stagewise power and expected stopping time for a drift \(\theta\), the mean of \(Z\) at full information. `drift_for_power` solves for the drift that gives the target power.

The example designs a one-sided 2.5% trial with 90% power and four equally spaced looks under three spending functions. The maximum information each design needs is expressed relative to a fixed design.

```{cpp, id=group-sequential, file="figures/group_sequential.svg", results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/plot/plot.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/distributions.hpp>
#include <datamunge/stats/group_sequential.hpp>

#include <cmath>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <random>
#include <vector>

namespace dm = datamunge;
using dm::stats::SpendingFunction;

int main() {
    const std::vector<double> looks{.25, .5, .75, 1};
    const double fixed_drift = dm::random::normal_quantile(.975) + dm::random::normal_quantile(.9);

    // Unadjusted repeated testing at 1.96.
    const auto naive = dm::stats::boundary_crossing_probabilities(looks, {1.96, 1.96, 1.96, 1.96}, 0);
    std::println("Four unadjusted looks at z = 1.96 have one-sided size {:.4f}.\n", naive.total);

    struct Design { const char* name; dm::stats::AlphaSpending spending; dm::graphics::RGBA color; };
    const std::vector<Design> designs{
        {"O'Brien–Fleming", {.function = SpendingFunction::obrien_fleming}, {26, 90, 147}},
        {"Power ρ = 3", {.function = SpendingFunction::power, .parameter = 3}, {46, 139, 87}},
        {"Pocock", {.function = SpendingFunction::pocock}, {176, 72, 43}},
    };
    std::println("| Spending | z at looks 1–4 | Final p | Max. info "
                 "| Mean, H1 | Mean, H0 |");
    std::println("| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    std::mt19937_64 random(5);
    std::normal_distribution<double> normal;
    std::vector<double> simulated;
    for (const auto& d : designs) {
        const auto b = dm::stats::group_sequential_boundaries(looks, d.spending);
        const double drift = dm::stats::drift_for_power(looks, b.z, .9);
        const double inflation = drift * drift / (fixed_drift * fixed_drift);
        const auto h1 = dm::stats::boundary_crossing_probabilities(looks, b.z, drift);
        const auto h0 = dm::stats::boundary_crossing_probabilities(looks, b.z, 0);
        std::println("| {} | {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f} | {:.4f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} | {:.3f} |",
                     d.name, b.z[0], b.z[1], b.z[2], b.z[3], b.nominal_alpha[3], inflation,
                     inflation * h1.expected_fraction, inflation * h0.expected_fraction);
        plot.line(looks, b.z, d.name, d.color, 1.8).points(looks, b.z, "", d.color, 6);

        // Monte Carlo check of the size: Brownian motion observed at the four looks.
        const int trials = 400000;
        int rejected = 0;
        for (int r = 0; r < trials; ++r) {
            double score = 0, previous = 0;
            for (std::size_t k = 0; k < looks.size(); ++k) {
                score += std::sqrt(looks[k] - previous) * normal(random);
                previous = looks[k];
                if (score / std::sqrt(looks[k]) >= b.z[k]) {
                    ++rejected;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        simulated.push_back(double(rejected) / trials);
    }
    std::println("\nSimulated size from 400,000 null trials: O'Brien–Fleming {:.4f}, power {:.4f}, "
                 "Pocock {:.4f}.", simulated[0], simulated[1], simulated[2]);

    plot.line({.25, 1}, {1.96, 1.96}, "Fixed-design 1.96", {150, 154, 160}, 1)
        .title("One-sided efficacy boundaries, α = 0.025, four looks")
        .x_label("Information fraction")
        .y_label("Critical value for Z")
        .size(700, 420);
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    plot.save_svg("build/group_sequential.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Four unadjusted looks at z = 1.96 have one-sided size 0.0631.

| Spending | z at looks 1–4 | Final p | Max. info | Mean, H1 | Mean, H0 |
| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| O'Brien–Fleming | 4.333, 2.963, 2.359, 2.014 | 0.0220 | 1.018 | 0.777 | 1.015 |
| Power ρ = 3 | 3.359, 2.760, 2.359, 2.029 | 0.0212 | 1.025 | 0.751 | 1.021 |
| Pocock | 2.368, 2.368, 2.358, 2.350 | 0.0094 | 1.178 | 0.697 | 1.164 |

Simulated size from 400,000 null trials: O'Brien–Fleming 0.0251, power 0.0255, Pocock 0.0249.
\image(figures/group_sequential.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Critical values for the standardized statistic at the four looks under O'Brien–Fleming-type, power (ρ = 3) and Pocock-type spending, with the fixed-design value 1.96 for reference.)
\alttext(The O'Brien–Fleming boundary falls steeply from 4.33 to 2.01, the power-family boundary falls from 3.36 to 2.03, and the Pocock boundary stays flat near 2.36. All of them lie above 1.96.)

All three designs have overall size 2.5%. The boundaries and stagewise probabilities agree with multivariate normal probabilities from `mvtnorm` (Miwa algorithm) to about \(10^{-7}\), and with `ldbounds` in R to the accuracy of its grid. The simulated sizes agree to within Monte Carlo error. The information columns are multiples of the information a fixed-sample design needs for 90% power. "Max. info" is the inflation factor, and the two "Mean" columns give the average information used at stopping under the alternative and under the null.

The spending functions trade the maximum against the average. O'Brien–Fleming-type spending keeps the early boundaries very high, so it almost never stops a trial on early noise. Its final critical value, 2.014, is close to 1.96, and it needs only 1.8% more information than a fixed design. Under the alternative, early stopping still saves about 22% of the fixed sample size on average. Pocock-type spending stops most quickly when the treatment works, using 70% of the fixed sample size on average. However, it needs 18% more information if the trial runs to the end, and its final look must reach \(p<0.0094\). The power family with \(\rho=3\) falls between the two. Under the null, every design runs past the fixed sample size, because efficacy-only boundaries rarely stop early. Adding a futility boundary is the usual remedy.

[Lan and DeMets (1983)|https://doi.org/10.1093/biomet/70.3.659] · [Armitage, McPherson and Rowe (1969)|https://doi.org/10.2307/2343787] · [Jennison and Turnbull (2000)|https://doi.org/10.1201/9781584888581] · [R ldbounds|https://cran.r-project.org/package=ldbounds]
